---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const sources = [
  {
    expert: 'Dr. Becky Kennedy',
    handle: '@drbeckyatgoodinside',
    title: 'The 15-second pause',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYFKYxKChCt/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'For conflict that escalates into meltdowns. Teach a timed pause: stop touching, move apart, breathe, check in with each other — then resume. Start at 15 seconds, build to 30, then a minute. It\'s a scaffold for self-regulation, not a punishment or a rigid rule. Kids won\'t do it perfectly; the habit is the point. Parents should also step back from solving every conflict so kids get reps at problem-solving.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Dr. Becky Kennedy',
    handle: '@drbeckyatgoodinside',
    title: 'Reduce arguments without saying "be nice"',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DdRSeZXAtqt/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: '"Be nice" fails because anger temporarily blocks a child\'s access to their own kindness — the capacity is there, the access isn\'t. Use a "side door": watch shows where siblings model kindness (she uses Gabby\'s Dollhouse and its sisters), name it lightly later — "sometimes it\'s hard" — and don\'t demand an instant apology or an immediate response. Validating the frustration makes kind behavior more likely afterward.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Dr. Becky Kennedy',
    handle: 'via @parentingcapital / @simplyparented',
    title: 'It\'s not about the toy — it\'s about attachment',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/Db8hFwVIad9/',
    format: 'Reel · live talk (Sweetwater / Marin Horizon)',
    summary: 'Sibling meanness is rarely about toys, sharing, or manners. Children are wired to monitor their attachment to parents — connection means food, shelter, safety — so a sibling registers as a rival for that resource. The single highest-leverage intervention is regular, phone-free one-on-one time with each child: "connection capital building on steroids." Level with kids about jealousy instead of shaming it, and never sort them into "the good kid" / "the bad kid." Also on @simplyparented: https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dc7PhMhP-UA/',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Dr. Becky Kennedy',
    handle: 'via @twoparentsandapod',
    title: 'Fair is not equal',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXunKfzk2vm/',
    format: 'Reel · podcast clip',
    summary: 'A well-meaning obsession with fairness is one of the biggest drivers of rivalry. If you read three-and-a-half books with one child, you do not owe the other three-and-a-half books — matching teaches kids to scan what their sibling got and demand the same, which scales into "my friend has X, so I need X" and lifelong competitiveness. Teach instead: in this family, each person gets what they need.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Dr. Becky Kennedy',
    handle: 'via @heartful_spoon (Jefferson Fisher interview)',
    title: 'Let kids borrow each other\'s traits',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/Ddl0mAhTb6l/',
    format: 'Reel · interview clip',
    summary: 'Siblings with opposite temperaments — the "easy," compliant one and the "fiery," assertive one — are not fixed types. In a family system there\'s roughly 100% of a trait to go around: if one child hogs generosity, the other gets none. Labels ("why can\'t you be like your brother?") lock the extremes in. Help the "good" child practice assertiveness (keeping the red cup instead of always giving it away) and the "difficult" child practice flexibility, playfully, through role-swaps.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Dr. Becky Kennedy — Good Inside, Ch. 15',
    handle: 'summarized by @eccchicago',
    title: 'Sibling Rivalry: the New Shoes story',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/p/DcRfx9RlHdO/',
    format: 'Carousel · 10 slides',
    summary: 'One child gets new shoes; the sibling protests — even though their shoes still fit. The protest is rarely about shoes. Underneath it is: "Do I matter as much? Will you notice what I need too?" The Good Inside answer: "We don\'t do fair. We do individual needs." Say what the other child needed, hold the boundary, and let disappointment exist — "the child is allowed to feel disappointed; the boundary stays." Practical tools: PNP time (short, phone-free, child-led one-on-one play) and giving jealousy somewhere safe to go — anger can be heard, insults cannot. When everyone is safe, slow the conflict down before deciding who is right.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Big Little Feelings',
    handle: '@biglittlefeelings',
    title: 'Coach, don\'t referee',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaBFtHCOXC2/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'The "major key" to stopping sibling fighting: stop refereeing. A referee takes sides — "you\'re wrong, give it back" — and both kids learn to perform for the verdict. A coach/narrator stays neutral: observe out loud, validate what\'s happening, then hand the problem back — "What are you guys going to do about it?" Kids are often surprisingly capable of solving it. Conflict itself is the curriculum: disagreeing, sharing power, working through frustration, repairing. Step in for safety — separate, create space — not for verdicts.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Big Little Feelings',
    handle: '@biglittlefeelings',
    title: 'When fighting crosses the line',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/p/DbMn-31G4qK/',
    format: 'Carousel · 6 slides',
    summary: 'A more serious post: most parents were never taught where normal fighting ends and abuse begins. The warning pattern is cumulative — one child keeps getting hurt, you\'re constantly refereeing, the same child always ends up hurt or scared, a child may be living in fear at home, and adults dismiss it with "they\'re just siblings." Points to a research-backed Substack series on protecting the targeted child and changing the family dynamic.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Devon Kuntzman · Transforming Toddlerhood',
    handle: '@transformingtoddlerhood',
    title: 'Stop the hurt, regulate, then teach',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbeZTmGhprm/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'The crucial toddler correction to "let them work it out": parents swing between yelling/punishing and ignoring/hoping — and both backfire, because toddlers are not developmentally equipped to resolve conflict alone. Their words, frustration tolerance, and impulse control are still under construction. Her 3 steps: 1) Stop the hurt — move in close, separate bodies, "I won\'t let you bite. Biting hurts," check the hurt child first. 2) Regulate — help both kids calm down; no forced apologies, no lectures to an overwhelmed brain. 3) Teach the missing skill once calm — "You wanted the toy. Try: \'Can I have a turn?\'" Kids learn conflict resolution because a parent kept them safe, helped them regulate, and coached them — repeatedly.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Dr. Siggie Cohen',
    handle: '@dr.siggie',
    title: 'Don\'t take sides',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/p/DeJxx_gm--v/',
    format: 'Carousel · 9 slides',
    summary: 'The headline rule is "Don\'t Take Sides" — which does not mean ignore unsafe behavior or skip discipline. Hear every perspective and guide each child instead of assigning blame. The child who "started it" needs help expressing a need appropriately; the "victim" needs support to stand up for themselves — not pity, which can lock in the role. Contrast: taking sides sounds like "Give that back — now he\'s crying"; neutral guidance addresses both children\'s part in getting to a next step.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Dr. Willough Jenkins',
    handle: '@drwilloughjenkins',
    title: 'Rivalry vs. bullying — look for fear',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcjZMPnuj6n/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'A child psychiatrist\'s distinction: normal rivalry is mutual, situational, and blows over. Bullying is one-directional, targets the same child repeatedly, and escalates. The most missed sign is fear — a child avoiding being alone with a sibling, dreading coming home, or showing anxiety around them. Cites Tucker et al., Pediatrics (2013) on sibling aggression\'s effects.',
  },
  {
    expert: 'Dr. Deborah Tillman',
    handle: '@drdeborahpurposedparenting',
    title: 'Hitting stops when the consequence is immediate',
    url: 'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DYqQoT-lFzK/',
    format: 'Reel',
    summary: 'The strictest voice in this set — a useful counterpoint. Hitting signals a missing, clear rule for unsafe hands, and without an immediate consequence the behavior continues. Step 1: move in and physically separate with no lectures. Then state the rule and apply the consequence right away, every time. Where Dr. Becky and Big Little Feelings emphasize coaching and connection first, Tillman emphasizes speed and consistency of the consequence for physical aggression specifically.',
  },
];
---
<Layout title="How to Get Siblings to Stop Fighting — Parenting" description="Dr. Becky Kennedy, Big Little Feelings, Dr. Siggie and more, summarized from their Instagram posts and reels: why siblings fight, what to say in the moment, scripts, and the red flags for bullying.">
  <main class="bg-[#0c0b09] min-h-screen px-4 py-14 sm:py-20 text-[#ece5d3]">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
      <a href="/parenting" class="text-sm text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline">← All parenting topics</a>
      <p class="mt-6 text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.35em] text-[#c9a86a]">Topic 01 · Sisters &amp; siblings</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight leading-[1.05] text-[#f7f0dc]">How to get siblings to stop fighting</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] max-w-2xl">
        Summarized from <strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">12 Instagram posts and reels</strong> by Dr. Becky Kennedy (Good Inside),
        Big Little Feelings, Dr. Siggie, Devon Kuntzman, Dr. Willough Jenkins, and Dr. Deborah Tillman.
        Every summary below links to the original — watch the reel, then use the script.
      </p>

      <!-- TL;DR -->
      <section class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-[#c9a86a]/40 bg-[#c9a86a]/10 p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-[#c9a86a]">The short answer</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-xl leading-relaxed text-[#f7f0dc] font-medium">You don't stop the fighting. You stop being the referee of it — and you drain the rivalry that fuels it.</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2.5 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">
          <li>• <strong class="text-white">In the moment:</strong> safety first, narrate don't judge, hold one calm boundary, separate if it's physical. No verdict, no forced apology, no "be nice."</li>
          <li>• <strong class="text-white">Between fights:</strong> daily phone-free one-on-one time with each child. Every expert lands here. It's the #1 lever for how often they fight.</li>
          <li>• <strong class="text-white">The mindset:</strong> it's almost never about the toy. It's "Do I matter as much as she does?" Answer that question and the toy fights shrink.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Why -->
      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Why they actually fight</h2>
        <div class="mt-5 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] text-[17px]">
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">1. Attachment, not objects — Dr. Becky.</strong> Children are wired to track their connection to you because, evolutionarily, connection <em>is</em> survival: food, shelter, safety. A sister isn't a playmate who took a cup; she's a competitor for the resource that keeps a child safe. That's why the intensity is so wildly out of proportion to the cup. Kids are constantly, unconsciously asking: what gets me more connection, and what threatens it?</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">2. "Do I matter as much?" — the New Shoes test.</strong> In Good Inside's sibling-rivalry chapter, one child gets new shoes and the other melts down despite her own shoes fitting fine. The protest translates to: <em>"Do I matter as much? Will you notice what I need, too?"</em> If you hear that question underneath the fight, your response changes completely — you answer the fear, not the footwear.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">3. Scorekeeping is taught — the fairness trap.</strong> Dr. Becky argues our obsession with being "fair" manufactures rivalry. Match every treat, book, and minute exactly, and you train both kids to audit each other's lives. The alternative family rule is needs, not sameness: different kids get different things at different times, and there is enough care for both.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">4. Fixed roles harden — borrow traits.</strong> Label one sister "the easy one" and the other "the difficult one" and the system defends those roles: in a family, a trait behaves like it has 100% to go around. The compliant child never practices saying no; the fiery one never practices bending. Fighting is often two half-skills colliding.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">5. Missing skills, especially under 5 — Devon Kuntzman.</strong> Toddlers and young preschoolers fight because the machinery isn't built yet: words for wanting, tolerance for waiting, brakes for impulses. Expecting a 2- and a 4-year-old to "work it out" is expecting a skill neither brain has. They borrow <em>your</em> regulation until theirs arrives.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- In the moment -->
      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">In the moment: the 60-second playbook</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[#a99f88] leading-relaxed">Synthesized from all six experts. They agree on the order, even when they differ on tone.</p>
        <ol class="mt-6 space-y-4">
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">1 · Safety before everything.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">If it's physical, move in and separate bodies first — calmly, with few words: "I won't let you hit. Hitting hurts." Check the hurt child. No investigation while anyone is swinging. (Unanimous — Kuntzman, Tillman, Siggie, BLF.)</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">2 · Regulate yourself out loud.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">Your calm is contagious in both directions. Dr. Becky's timer image helps: you're not failing because you want to jump in — that urge is normal. Breathe first, speak second.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">3 · Narrate, don't referee.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">Big Little Feelings + Dr. Siggie: describe what you see without a verdict. "I see two kids who both want the red cup, and you're both mad." No "who started it," no "give it back, she's crying." Taking sides teaches performing for the judge; narrating teaches seeing the problem.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">4 · One boundary, held kindly.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">"You can be angry. You can't grab." / "You're allowed to be disappointed. The boundary stays." Feelings are unlimited; behavior has edges. Don't soften the boundary because the feeling is big — that's the whole lesson.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">5 · Insert Dr. Becky's pause.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">Stop touching, step apart, breathe, check in: "Are you okay? Are we ready?" Start with 15 seconds. Over weeks, stretch to 30, then 60. You're installing a gap between feeling and action that they will use everywhere, not just with each other.</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">6 · Hand it back — age permitting.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">For verbal fights between older preschoolers and up: "What are you two going to do about it?" Then actually wait. For toddlers, skip this step and coach the words yourself — they can't do this part yet (Kuntzman's correction).</span></li>
          <li class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5"><strong class="text-[#f7f0dc]">7 · Teach later, when brains are back.</strong> <span class="text-[#c9c0ad]">No lectures mid-storm and no forced "say sorry." Once calm — minutes or hours later — practice the missing skill in one line: "You wanted a turn. Next time try: 'My turn next?'" Repair (a drawing, a rebuilt tower, a real apology) happens here, not under duress.</span></li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <!-- Scripts -->
      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Scripts you can copy</h2>
        <div class="mt-6 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[#c9a86a]">The toy grab</p>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">"I see you both want this. I'm going to hold it while we figure it out. [Older]: you had it first — you'll get it back. [Younger]: waiting is so hard. I'll wait with you."</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[#c9a86a]">Hitting</p>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">"I won't let you hit your sister. Hitting hurts. You are mad — mad is okay. I'm moving you over here with me until our bodies are calm." <span class="text-[#8a8474]">(Then check the hurt sister first, briefly and warmly — not theatrically.)</span></p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[#c9a86a]">"That's not fair! She got one!"</p>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">"In our family we don't do fair — we do what each person needs. She needed new shoes because hers pinched. When you need something, you'll get it, even if she doesn't get one that day. It's okay to feel disappointed about that."</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[#c9a86a]">Instead of "be nice" / "say sorry"</p>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">"You're really frustrated with her right now. I get it — sisters can be annoying." <span class="text-[#8a8474]">(Later, calmly:)</span> "That was a hard moment. What could we do to make it right?"</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[#c9a86a]">Instead of "why can't you be like your sister?"</p>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">Never compare — it welds the roles in place. Name the individual need instead: "You needed it quiet and she needed it loud. Let's find a way for both needs to fit in this house."</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[#c9a86a]">The handoff (ages ~4+)</p>
            <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">"I hear two kids with a problem. I trust you can figure out a way that works for both of you. I'll be right here if it gets too big."</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Between fights -->
      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Between fights: what actually reduces how often they fight</h2>
        <div class="mt-5 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] text-[17px]">
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">PNP time / connection capital — the consensus #1.</strong> Dr. Becky calls short, regular, phone-free one-on-one time "connection capital building on steroids" and the first intervention for <em>any</em> behavior problem. Good Inside's version: brief child-led play, no phone, no sibling, no agenda. Ten focused minutes per child per day outperforms an hour of distracted togetherness, because it directly answers the "do I matter as much?" question before it has to be asked at full volume.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Preach needs, not fairness — at calm moments.</strong> Don't debut "fair ≠ equal" mid-meltdown. Plant it on ordinary days: narrate your own uneven giving ("Today sister got the extra story because she was sad. Tomorrow might be your turn for an extra something, because you'll need it then.")</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Give jealousy somewhere safe to go.</strong> Make it sayable: "It's okay to wish the baby would go back." / "You can tell me you're mad at your sister. I can handle hearing it. I won't let you hurt her, and I won't shame you for feeling it." Jealousy that can be spoken gets acted out less.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Use the side door.</strong> Stories and shows where siblings clash and repair (Dr. Becky's Gabby's Dollhouse example) let you rehearse kindness and repair at zero emotional cost. Name it once, lightly, later. Don't quiz.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Lend traits across the aisle.</strong> Deliberately give the "easy" sister chances to keep, choose, and refuse — and the "fiery" sister playful chances to yield and flex. Praise the borrowed trait specifically when you see it, in private, not as a comparison.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Don't optimize away every discomfort.</strong> A wider Dr. Becky principle that runs under all of this: rescuing kids from every friction — wrong class, lost game, sister got there first — buys relief today at the price of fragility later. The struggle, survived with you nearby, is where capability comes from.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Sisters note -->
      <section class="mt-14 rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/[0.04] p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">A note for sisters — and for a big age gap</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">Nothing in the expert consensus is sister-specific — the attachment and fairness dynamics are the same for brothers — but two findings bite hardest with sisters close in age: the <em>comparison</em> loop ("why can't you be like your sister?") and the <em>fixed-role</em> loop (the good one / the difficult one) both intensify when two girls are constantly measured against each other. Guard the language: describe behavior and needs, never rank the children.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">With a toddler in the mix, weight Kuntzman over Big Little Feelings: a 2-year-old cannot narrate, negotiate, or "borrow your calm" for long. With a big gap, weight Dr. Siggie: the older child must not become the default giver-upper ("you're older, you should know better" breeds the exact resentment that fuels the next fight), and the younger must not learn that small = excused. Each child is responsible for their own behavior, at their own level.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Disagreements -->
      <section class="mt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Where the experts disagree</h2>
        <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] text-[17px]">
          <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">How much to step back:</strong> Big Little Feelings' coach model assumes kids can often solve it if you narrate and wait. Kuntzman says that's true for older preschoolers, false for toddlers — under-5s need you in close, teaching, every time. Both agree on the destination; they disagree on the starting age.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Consequences for hitting:</strong> Dr. Tillman wants an immediate, consistent consequence every time, or the rule isn't real. Dr. Becky / BLF put the weight on separation, boundary, and later skill-building, and warn against punishment that shames. Practical middle most parents land on: immediate separation + a brief, boring, related consequence, delivered without anger.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Forced closeness as repair:</strong> One widely-shared family-therapy approach (Michella Meier-Morsi's viral post) has fighting siblings hold hands for 10 minutes until they're friends again — commenters split sharply, some reporting lifelong closeness, others flagging forced physical contact during conflict. The experts above mostly favor space first, closeness by choice after calm.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Red flags -->
      <section class="mt-14 rounded-2xl border border-red-400/30 bg-red-400/[0.06] p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">When it's more than rivalry</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">From Big Little Feelings and child psychiatrist Dr. Willough Jenkins — get support (pediatrician, child psychologist, school counselor) if you see a pattern, not a bad day:</p>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 leading-relaxed text-[#ddd3ba]">
          <li>• It's <strong>one-directional</strong> — the same child is always the target, and the same child always the aggressor.</li>
          <li>• The targeted child shows <strong>fear</strong>: avoiding being alone with their sibling, dreading coming home, anxiety around them.</li>
          <li>• It <strong>escalates</strong> over time instead of blowing over, or injuries become regular.</li>
          <li>• One child seems to <strong>live scared at home</strong>, and adults (including you) are tempted to file it under "they're just siblings."</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-[#a99f88]">Normal rivalry is mutual, situational, and blows over. Bullying is one-directional, repeated, and escalating. The difference is the pattern — and fear is the tell.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Sources -->
      <section class="mt-16">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">The sources, summarized one by one</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[#a99f88]">Everything above is drawn from these Instagram posts and reels. Summaries are in our words; the originals are the experts'.</p>
        <div class="mt-8 grid gap-5">
          {sources.map((s, i) => (
            <article class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-6">
              <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
                <span class="text-[#c9a86a] font-bold">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">{s.title}</h3>
                <span class="text-xs rounded-full border border-white/15 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[#c9c0ad]">{s.format}</span>
              </div>
              <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">{s.expert} · {s.handle}</p>
              <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">{s.summary}</p>
              <a href={s.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="mt-3 inline-block text-sm text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline break-all">Watch / read on Instagram ↗ {s.url}</a>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
